From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ribbon OS maintainers <ribbon@localhost>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 23:59:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add -passfds: receive file descriptors passed with
 SCM_RIGHTS

A descriptor passed with SCM_RIGHTS travels with the bytes of the
sendmsg that carried it, and the kernel hands it to the first read that
takes some of those bytes. inputProc reads with read(), which can't take
it, so the kernel closes it. Tcl reads ahead, so a gets or a readable
event can lose descriptors that came with bytes the script hasn't looked
at yet.

unix_sockets::connect path ?-passfds bool? and
unix_sockets::listen path handler ?-passfds bool? make the connection
read with recvmsg instead. Each message's descriptors are queued on the
channel, tagged with the stream offset where the message that carried
them ended. That offset is exact, because the kernel ends a read right
after a message that carries descriptors.

unix_sockets::fds chan ?-all? ?-messages? hands out the descriptors of
messages the script has read (or, with -all, all received) as new
channels, so gets, read and chan event work as before. Descriptors never
taken are closed with the channel. If the open file limit made the
kernel drop some, fds fails once with UNIX_SOCKETS CTRUNC instead of
losing them silently.

It is an option at creation, not a settable channel option, because
descriptors read before it was turned on would already be gone.
chan configure shows two read-only options: -passfds, and -fdqueue (the
received byte count and the queue, with fd numbers that stay owned by
the channel). -fdqueue lets another extension take the connection over
by duplicating the queued descriptors, without linking against this one.

The default is unchanged. Tests uds-8.* use python3's socket.send_fds as
the sending peer, and are skipped without it.
