+**unix_sockets::fds** *channel* ?**-all**? ?**-messages**?
+
+:   Take the file descriptors queued on a connection opened with
+    **-passfds 1** and return a new channel for each, in the order they
+    were sent.  Without **-all**, only those of messages the script has
+    read in full; with **-all**, every one received so far, including
+    those of a message still in Tcl's input buffer.  With **-messages**
+    the result is a list of *offset* {*channel* ...} pairs, one per
+    message, instead of a flat list of channels.  Returns an empty list
+    when nothing is queued; it never blocks or reads.
+
+
+## PASSING FILE DESCRIPTORS
+
+A process can send open file descriptors over a unix socket with
+**sendmsg**(2) and **SCM_RIGHTS**.  They travel with the bytes of that
+**sendmsg**, and the kernel hands them to the first read that takes some
+of those bytes.  A plain **read**(2) can't take them, so the kernel
+closes them.  That is what happens by default, because Tcl reads ahead
+into its buffer: a **gets** or a readable event can discard descriptors
+sent with bytes the script has not even looked at.
+
+The **-passfds 1** option makes the channel read with **recvmsg**(2)
+instead, so no descriptor is discarded.  Each message's descriptors are
+queued on the channel, tagged with the stream offset (bytes since the
+connection was made) where the message that carried them ended.  The
+kernel ends a read right after a message that carries descriptors, so the
+offset is exact, unless the message is larger than the channel's
+**-buffersize**: then its descriptors arrive with its first part, and the
+offset is where that part ended.
+
+So **gets**, **read** and **chan event** work as usual, and
+**unix_sockets::fds** hands the descriptors out once the script has read
+the message they came with.  A protocol that knows how many descriptors a
+message carries (Wayland, D-Bus) can just as well take them in order.
+Received descriptors are close-on-exec, and descriptors that are never
+taken are closed with the channel.  If the kernel could not pass all of a
+message's descriptors (the open file limit was reached), the first
+**unix_sockets::fds** that reaches that message fails once with
+errorCode {UNIX_SOCKETS CTRUNC}, and the next call returns those that did
+arrive.  On a channel without **-passfds 1** it fails with errorCode
+{UNIX_SOCKETS PASSFDS}.
+
+Without the option nothing changes.  The option can't be changed later
+(**-passfds** is read-only in **chan configure**), since descriptors read
+before it was turned on would already be lost.
+
+These channels have two read-only options:
+
+**-passfds**
+
+:   1 if the channel receives passed descriptors, else 0.  On a listening
+    socket, whether the connections it accepts do.
+
+**-fdqueue**
+
+:   For other extensions that take a connection over (they duplicate the
+    descriptors, which stay owned by this channel).  Empty without
+    **-passfds 1**, otherwise a list: the number of bytes read from the
+    socket so far, then one {*offset* *lost* {*fd* ...}} element per
+    queued message, oldest first.
 
 
 ## EXAMPLES
@@ -79,10 +145,22 @@ vwait ::done
 ```
 
 
+A client of a server that answers "OPEN *name*" with a line, sent with
+the opened file's descriptor:
+
+```tcl
+set con [unix_sockets::connect /tmp/files.socket -passfds 1]
+puts $con "OPEN image.squashfs"
+flush $con
+gets $con status
+set image [lindex [unix_sockets::fds $con] 0]
+```
+
+
